WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.933
CORS and the preflight: why the browser, not your server, decides who may read an answer.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:14.771
Your front end runs on localhost, port 5173. Your API runs on localhost, port 3000.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:22.774
Same machine, different origins. An origin is scheme, host and port. Change any one, and it's another origin.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.363
A page's JavaScript may freely read answers only from its own origin. That's the same-origin policy.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.704
So the page sends a plain GET to the API.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.569
It goes out. The server receives it, runs the route, and answers.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.073
Your Express terminal even logs the GET. The request arrived.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:49.075
But the answer stops at the browser. The page can't read it, unless the server allows it.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.632
Every answer can carry a permission slip: Access-Control-Allow-Origin.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:03.000
If it names the page's origin, the browser hands the answer over. If it's missing, or names someone else, the page gets a CORS error.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:08.783
In Express, that's one line: app.use(cors({ origin })), with the origin of your front end.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.567
Now the page deletes task 7, with a token in the Authorization header.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:21.855
That's not a simple request. So first, the browser phones ahead: an OPTIONS request called the preflight.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.510
It asks: may this origin use DELETE, with an Authorization header?

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.608
Only if the answer says yes does the real request cross.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.769
But the server allows only GET and POST.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:43.009
The browser blocks the call. Chrome's console says: Method DELETE is not allowed by Access-Control-Allow-Methods in preflight response.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:47.396
The DELETE was never sent. Task 7 is still there.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.502
Add DELETE to methods.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:56.048
The preflight passes. The real DELETE crosses, and the server answers 204.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.762
In the Network tab you'll see two rows: OPTIONS first, then DELETE.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:08.254
And mount cors first. A preflight carries no token, so auth would answer 401, and the preflight would fail.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.388
Notice who did the blocking. The browser, not the server.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:18.207
curl and Postman never ask permission, so they never see a CORS error.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:25.953
CORS doesn't protect your API either. Anyone outside a browser can still call it. Authentication does that.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:35.947
One more case: cookies. The page asks with credentials: include, and the server must name the exact origin, never a star (*), and allow credentials.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.421
A Bearer token in a header needs none of this.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.267
Practise it with the CORS simulator in Authentication and security.
